y": "safe"}, "sexual": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "violence": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}}}]}, "input": {"messages": [{"content": "\nYou are an AI assistant that helps a human analyst to perform general information discovery. Information discovery is the process of identifying and assessing relevant information associated with certain entities (e.g., organizations and individuals) within a network.\n\n# Goal\nWrite a comprehensive report of a community, given a list of entities that belong to the community as well as their relationships and optional associated claims. The report will be used to inform decision-makers about information associated with the community and their potential impact. The content of this report includes an overview of the community's key entities, their legal compliance, technical capabilities, reputation, and noteworthy claims.\n\n# Report Structure\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insights about the community. Each insight should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded according to the grounding rules below. Be comprehensive.\n\nReturn output as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\n# Example Input\n-----------\nText:\n\nEntities\n\nid,entity,description\n5,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n6,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is an organization that is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n\nRelationships\n\nid,source,target,description\n37,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n38,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n39,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,The Unity March is taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n40,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,TRIBUNE SPOTLIGHT,Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity march taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n41,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,BAILEY ASADI,Bailey Asadi is speaking at Verdant Oasis Plaza about the march\n43,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,UNITY MARCH,Harmony Assembly is organizing the Unity March\n\nOutput:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza and Unity March\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community revolves around the Verdant Oasis Plaza, which is the location of the Unity March. The plaza has relationships with the Harmony Assembly, Unity March, and Tribune Spotlight, all of which are associated with the march event.\",\n    \"rating\": 5.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the potential for unrest or conflict during the Unity March.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza as the central location\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza is the central entity in this community, serving as the location for the Unity March. This plaza is the common link between all other entities, suggesting its significance in the community. The plaza's association with the march could potentially lead to issues such as public disorder or conflict, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. [Data: Entities (5), Relationships (37, 38, 39, 40, 41,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Harmony Assembly's role in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Harmony Assembly is another key entity in this community, being the organizer of the march at Verdant Oasis Plaza. The nature of Harmony Assembly and its march could be a potential source of threat, depending on their objectives and the reactions they provoke. The relationship between Harmony Assembly and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Entities(6), Relationships (38, 43)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Unity March as a significant event\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Unity March is a significant event taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza. This event is a key factor in the community's dynamics and could be a potential source of threat, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. The relationship between the march and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Relationships (39)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Tribune Spotlight\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity March taking place in Verdant Oasis Plaza. This suggests that the event has attracted media attention, which could amplify its impact on the community. The role of Tribune Spotlight could be significant in shaping public perception of the event and the entities involved. [Data: Relationships (40)]\"\n        }\n    ]\n}\n\n\n# Real Data\n\nUse the following text for your answer. Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n984,PREDICTED VALUES,\"**Predicted Values: Comprehensive Summary**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nPredicted values are the outcomes generated from statistical models that estimate the expected performance or behavior of a system based on input variables. They serve as a crucial component in various experimental designs, including the Worsted Yarn Experiment and plasma etching experiments, where they help in understanding the relationship between independent variables and the response variable.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure:**\nPredicted values are typically derived from regression models, which can be expressed in a general form as:\n\\[ \\hat{Y} = f(X_1, X_2, ..., X_k) \\]\nwhere \\( \\hat{Y} \\) represents the predicted value, and \\( X_1, X_2, ..., X_k \\) are the independent variables. The function \\( f \\) can take various forms, including linear, polynomial, or other types of regression models depending on the nature of the data and the relationships being modeled.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nIn the context of experimental designs, predicted values are generated from the data collected during the experiments. The design layout may include factors and levels that are systematically varied to observe their effects on the response variable. For instance, in a factorial design, all combinations of factor levels are tested, and the predicted values are calculated based on the fitted model.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of predicted values relies on several assumptions, including:\n1. Linearity: The relationship between the independent variables and the response variable is linear.\n2. Independence: Observations are independent of one another.\n3. Homoscedasticity: The variance of residuals is constant across all levels of the independent variables.\n4. Normality: The residuals of the model are normally distributed.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nPredicted values are applicable in scenarios where a relationship between independent variables and a response variable is hypothesized. They are particularly useful when forecasting outcomes, optimizing processes, or validating experimental results. Selection criteria for using predicted values include the nature of the data, the complexity of the relationships, and the specific objectives of the analysis.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis procedure involves fitting a statistical model to the experimental data, generating predicted values, and comparing these values against actual observations to assess model performance. This comparison is often quantified using residuals, which are the differences between observed and predicted values.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Predicted values provide a quantitative estimate of outcomes based on statistical models, facilitating decision-making and forecasting.\n- They allow for the evaluation of model fit and the identification of significant predictors.\n- Predicted values can enhance understanding of complex relationships in data.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- The accuracy of predicted values is contingent upon the validity of the underlying model and its assumptions.\n- Overfitting can occur if the model is too complex, leading to poor generalization to new data.\n- Predicted values may not account for unobserved variables or interactions that could influence the response.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nPredicted values are closely related to various statistical methods, including linear regression, multiple regression, and generalized linear models. Alternatives may include machine learning techniques, such as decision trees or neural networks, which can also generate predictions based on input data.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing predicted values, researchers should ensure that the model is appropriately specified and validated. It is essential to conduct diagnostic checks on residuals to confirm that the assumptions of the model are met. Additionally, cross-validation techniques can be employed to assess the robustness of the predictions.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nPredicted values can be generated using various statistical software packages, including R, Python (with libraries such as scikit-learn), SAS, and SPSS. These tools provide functions for fitting models, generating predictions, and conducting residual analysis.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nPredicted values are widely used in fields such as manufacturing (e.g., predicting yarn performance), healthcare (e.g., forecasting patient outcomes), and environmental science (e.g., estimating pollutant levels). They are integral to any analysis where understanding the impact of multiple factors on a response variable is necessary.\",5\r\n619,GENERALIZED LINEAR MODEL,\"### Generalized Linear Model (GLM)\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nThe Generalized Linear Model (GLM) is a family of statistical models that extends traditional linear regression to accommodate response variables that follow distributions from the exponential family. This includes not only normal distributions but also binomial, Poisson, and gamma distributions, among others. The primary purpose of GLMs is to provide a unified framework for modeling various types of data, allowing for both linear and nonlinear relationships between the predictors and the response variable.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation**  \nThe structure of a GLM consists of three components:  \n1. **Random Component**: Specifies the probability distribution of the response variable \\( Y \\) (e.g., normal, binomial, Poisson).\n2. **Systematic Component**: A linear predictor \\( \\eta \\) defined as \\( \\eta = X\\beta \\), where \\( X \\) is the matrix of explanatory variables and \\( \\beta \\) is the vector of coefficients.\n3. **Link Function**: A function \\( g \\) that relates the mean of the response variable \\( \\mu \\) to the linear predictor, expressed as \\( g(\\mu) = \\eta \\). Common link functions include the logit link for binomial data and the log link for Poisson data.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nGLMs can be applied in various experimental designs, including completely randomized designs, factorial designs, and block designs. The choice of design depends on the nature of the data and the research question.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \n1. The response variable must follow a distribution from the exponential family.\n2. The observations must be independent.\n3. The link function must be correctly specified.\n4. The linear predictor must adequately capture the relationship between the predictors and the response.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nGLMs are applicable when the response variable is not normally distributed or when the relationship between the predictors and the response is nonlinear. They are particularly useful in situations involving count data, binary outcomes, or proportions. Selection criteria include the nature of the response variable, the distribution of the data, and the research objectives.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis of GLMs typically involves:\n1. Specifying the appropriate distribution and link function.\n2. Fitting the model using maximum likelihood estimation.\n3. Evaluating model fit through diagnostic plots and statistical tests (e.g., deviance, Akaike Information Criterion).\n4. Interpreting the coefficients in the context of the chosen link function.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \n- Flexibility in modeling various types of response variables.\n- Ability to handle non-normal data distributions.\n- Incorporation of different link functions allows for modeling complex relationships.\n- Provides a unified framework for both linear and nonlinear regression.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \n- Requires careful selection of the appropriate distribution and link function.\n- May be computationally intensive for large datasets or complex models.\n- Interpretation of coefficients can be less straightforward compared to ordinary least squares regression.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRelated methods include Generalized Additive Models (GAMs), which extend GLMs by allowing for non-linear relationships through smooth functions. Other alternatives include traditional linear regression for normally distributed data and non-parametric methods for non-standard distributions.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing GLMs, researchers should ensure that the data meets the assumptions of the model. It is also important to conduct exploratory data analysis to understand the distribution of the response variable and the relationships with predictors.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nGLMs are widely supported in statistical software packages such as R (using the `glm()` function), SAS (using the `PROC GENMOD` procedure), and Python (using the `statsmodels` library). These tools provide functionalities for fitting GLMs, conducting hypothesis tests, and generating diagnostic plots.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nGLMs are commonly used in various fields, including:\n- Medical research for modeling binary outcomes (e.g., presence/absence of a disease).\n- Ecology for analyzing count data (e.g., species abundance).\n- Social sciences for modeling survey data with categorical responses.\n- Marketing for analyzing conversion rates in A/B testing scenarios. \n\nIn summary, the Generalized Linear Model is a versatile and powerful tool in statistical modeling, allowing researchers to analyze a wide range of data types and relationships effectively.\",13\r\n2203,JOHNSON AND MONTGOMERY,\"**JOHNSON AND MONTGOMERY: Comprehensive Summary**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nJohnson and Montgomery are prominent authors in the field of Design of Experiments (DOE) and are particularly noted for their contributions to the development and application of optimal designs for generalized linear models (GLMs). Their work provides a framework for efficiently designing experiments that can lead to valid statistical inferences in various fields, including agriculture, engineering, and social sciences.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure:**\nWhile the specific mathematical formulations are not detailed in the provided descriptions, generalized linear models typically involve a response variable that is related to a linear predictor through a link function. The general form of a GLM can be expressed as:\n\\[ g(E(Y)) = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1X_1 + \\beta_2X_2 + ... + \\beta_kX_k \\]\nwhere \\( g \\) is the link function, \\( E(Y) \\) is the expected value of the response variable \\( Y \\), and \\( \\beta_i \\) are the coefficients associated with the predictors \\( X_i \\).\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nThe design layout for experiments involving GLMs can vary widely depending on the nature of the response variable and the factors involved. Johnson and Montgomery emphasize the importance of optimal designs, which are structured to minimize the variance of the estimated parameters and maximize the information gained from the experimental runs.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nFor the application of GLMs and the designs proposed by Johnson and Montgomery, several assumptions must be met:\n1. The response variable follows a distribution from the exponential family (e.g., normal, binomial, Poisson).\n2. The link function correctly relates the mean of the response to the linear predictors.\n3. Observations are independent.\n4. The model is correctly specified, including the appropriate choice of predictors and link function.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nJohnson and Montgomery's methodologies are applicable when researchers need to design experiments that involve response variables that do not conform to normality or have non-constant variance. Their work is particularly useful in situations where optimality criteria, such as D-optimality or A-optimality, are desired to enhance the efficiency of the experimental design.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis of data collected from experiments designed using Johnson and Montgomery's principles typically involves fitting a generalized linear model to the data using maximum likelihood estimation. The analysis may include hypothesis testing for the significance of predictors, model diagnostics, and validation of assumptions.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\nThe strengths of the approaches advocated by Johnson and Montgomery include:\n- The ability to handle a wide range of response distributions.\n- Enhanced efficiency in parameter estimation through optimal design strategies.\n- Flexibility in modeling complex relationships between predictors and responses.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\nSome limitations include:\n- The complexity of determining optimal designs, which may require advanced statistical software and computational resources.\n- The need for careful consideration of model assumptions, as violations can lead to misleading results.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated methodologies include traditional factorial designs, response surface methodology, and Bayesian experimental designs. Each of these approaches has its own strengths and is suitable for different types of experimental questions.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing designs based on Johnson and Montgomery's work, researchers should consider the availability of resources, the feasibility of conducting the required number of experimental runs, and the potential for confounding factors that may affect the results.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nWhile the descriptions do not specify software, common statistical software packages such as R, SAS, and Minitab provide functionalities for designing experiments and fitting generalized linear models, including tools for optimal design.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nJohnson and Montgomery's principles are widely applied in various fields, including clinical trials, agricultural experiments, and industrial quality control, where the response variable may be binary, count data, or continuous with non-constant variance. Their work serves as a foundational reference for researchers seeking to optimize their experimental designs in these contexts.\",2\r\n2204,MYERS ET AL.,\"**Comprehensive Summary of \"\"MYERS ET AL.\"\" in the Context of Experimental Design and Analysis**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nMyers et al. are recognized authors in the field of experimental design, particularly noted for their contributions to the development and application of generalized linear models (GLMs) and Response Surface Methodology (RSM). Their work serves as a foundational reference for researchers and practitioners seeking to design experiments that effectively model complex relationships between variables and optimize responses.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure:**\nWhile specific mathematical formulations are not detailed in the descriptions, generalized linear models typically involve a linear predictor that is linked to a response variable through a specified link function. In the context of RSM, the model often takes the form of a polynomial equation that approximates the response surface in the vicinity of an optimal point.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nMyers et al. emphasize the importance of structured experimental designs that facilitate the exploration of response surfaces. This includes the use of factorial designs, central composite designs, and Box-Behnken designs, which are integral to RSM. These designs allow for the systematic investigation of multiple factors and their interactions.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe application of generalized linear models and RSM relies on several key assumptions:\n1. The response variable follows a specific distribution (e.g., normal, binomial, Poisson).\n2. The relationship between the predictors and the response is appropriately modeled by the chosen link function.\n3. Independence of observations.\n4. Homoscedasticity (constant variance of errors).\n5. Linearity in the parameters for RSM.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nMyers et al. provide guidance on when to employ GLMs and RSM, suggesting their use in scenarios where:\n- The response variable is influenced by multiple factors.\n- The relationship between factors and responses is complex and nonlinear.\n- Optimization of responses is desired, particularly in industrial and agricultural applications.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis of data collected from experiments designed using Myers et al.'s methodologies typically involves:\n1. Fitting the appropriate GLM or RSM model to the data.\n2. Conducting hypothesis tests to evaluate the significance of factors and interactions.\n3. Utilizing diagnostic plots to assess model fit and assumptions.\n4. Performing optimization techniques to identify optimal factor levels.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\nThe methodologies advocated by Myers et al. offer several advantages:\n- Flexibility in modeling various types of response data.\n- Ability to explore interactions between factors.\n- Efficient use of resources through structured experimental designs.\n- Capability to identify optimal conditions for processes.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\nDespite their strengths, there are limitations associated with the approaches discussed by Myers et al.:\n- Complexity in model selection and interpretation, particularly with high-dimensional data.\n- Potential for overfitting if the model is too complex relative to the amount of data.\n- Assumptions may not hold in all practical situations, leading to biased results.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nMyers et al.'s work is closely related to other experimental design methodologies, including:\n- Factorial designs and fractional factorial designs for screening purposes.\n- Taguchi methods for robust design.\n- Bayesian optimization techniques for response surface exploration.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing the designs and methodologies proposed by Myers et al., practitioners should consider:\n- The scale and scope of the experiment to ensure adequate data collection.\n- The selection of appropriate software tools for analysis, such as R, SAS, or Minitab, which provide functionalities for GLMs and RSM.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nWhile specific software implementations are not mentioned in the descriptions, common statistical software packages like R, SAS, and Minitab are widely used for conducting analyses based on Myers et al.'s methodologies, offering built-in functions for GLMs and RSM.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nMyers et al.'s contributions are applicable in various fields, including:\n- Industrial process optimization.\n- Agricultural experiments to enhance crop yields.\n- Pharmaceutical research for dose-response studies.\n\nIn summary, Myers et al. provide a comprehensive framework for designing experiments and analyzing data using generalized linear models and response surface methodology, making significant contributions to the field of Design of Experiments.\",2\r\n2851,DATA TRANSFORMATION,\"### Comprehensive Summary of Data Transformation\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nData transformation is the process of converting data from one format or structure into another to enhance its usability for analysis. This process is crucial in preparing raw data for statistical analysis, ensuring that it meets the assumptions of various analytical methods and improving the overall quality of insights derived from the data.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation or Model Structure**  \nWhile data transformation does not have a singular mathematical formulation, it often involves operations such as normalization, standardization, logarithmic transformation, and polynomial transformation. Each of these methods alters the data's scale or distribution to meet specific analytical requirements.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nData transformation does not have a traditional design layout as seen in experimental designs. However, it can be conceptualized as a series of steps that include identifying the need for transformation, selecting the appropriate transformation method, applying the transformation, and validating the results.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nThe validity of data transformation relies on several assumptions, including:\n- The original data must be appropriately structured and relevant to the analysis.\n- The chosen transformation should align with the statistical methods to be applied, ensuring that assumptions such as normality, homoscedasticity, and linearity are satisfied.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nData transformation is applicable in various scenarios, including:\n- When the data exhibits skewness or non-normality, which can violate the assumptions of parametric tests.\n- When the scale of measurement is inappropriate for the intended analysis (e.g., using ratios instead of raw counts).\n- When enhancing the interpretability of the data or improving the performance of machine learning algorithms.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis procedure following data transformation typically involves:\n1. Assessing the original data for distribution and scale.\n2. Selecting an appropriate transformation method based on the analysis goals.\n3. Applying the transformation and re-evaluating the data characteristics.\n4. Conducting the intended statistical analysis on the transformed data.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \nThe advantages of data transformation include:\n- Improved compliance with statistical assumptions, leading to more reliable results.\n- Enhanced interpretability of data, making it easier to derive insights.\n- Increased model performance in predictive analytics, particularly in machine learning contexts.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \nLimitations of data transformation include:\n- Potential loss of information or interpretability if transformations are not carefully chosen.\n- The risk of overfitting in predictive models if transformations are too complex or tailored to specific datasets.\n- The possibility of introducing bias if the transformation is not representative of the underlying data distribution.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nData transformation is often related to other preprocessing methods such as data cleaning, feature selection, and dimensionality reduction techniques like Principal Component Analysis (PCA). Each of these methods can complement data transformation in preparing datasets for analysis.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing data transformation, practitioners should consider:\n- The context of the data and the specific analytical goals.\n- The potential impact of transformation on the interpretability of results.\n- The need for validation of transformed data to ensure that it meets the assumptions of subsequent analyses.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nData transformation can be performed using various statistical software packages, including R, Python (with libraries such as pandas and scikit-learn), SAS, and SPSS. These tools provide built-in functions for common transformations, making it easier for analysts to apply them effectively.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nCommon applications of data transformation include:\n- Logarithmic transformation of financial data to stabilize variance and normalize distributions.\n- Standardization of test scores to allow for comparison across different scales.\n- Normalization of image data in machine learning to improve model training efficiency.\n\nIn summary, data transformation is a fundamental process in data analysis that enhances the usability and interpretability of data, ensuring that it is suitable for various statistical methods and analytical techniques.\",2\r\n2201,POISSON REGRESSION,A special case of the generalized linear model used for count data where the response variable follows a Poisson distribution.,1\r\n2202,GOTWALT ET AL.,Authors of a method used to construct D-optimal designs for experiments involving generalized linear models.,1\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n2310,PREDICTED VALUES,RESIDUALS,\"Predicted values are used to calculate residuals, which reflect the accuracy of the model\",23\r\n3016,GENERALIZED LINEAR MODEL,JOHNSON AND MONTGOMERY,JOHNSON AND MONTGOMERY provided examples of designed experiments for generalized linear models.,15\r\n3017,GENERALIZED LINEAR MODEL,MYERS ET AL.,MYERS ET AL. provided examples of designed experiments for generalized linear models.,15\r\n4061,GENERALIZED LINEAR MODEL,DATA TRANSFORMATION,Data transformation is of